Silicon Valley has a long history of political pivots


Tesla and SpaceX CEO Elon Musk looks on as Donald Trump signs executive ord=
ers in the Oval Office of the White House in Washington DC on 11 February 2=
025. Photograph: Jim Watson/AFP/Getty Images


Jensen Huang isn=E2=80=99t the only Silicon Valley tycoon starting a new ch=
apter. Elon Musk, Palantir=E2=80=99s Alex Karp, and others in Silicon Valle=
y are turning a new leaf politically.

The wisdom among company founders goes that if your product finds no fit in=
 the market, you pivot your company to something else. Try again. Iterate.

Many of Silicon Valley=E2=80=99s leaders treat their politics in much the s=
ame way. Strike an alliance, milk it, iterate, pivot.

A significant number of the tech barons were major boosters of Barack Obama=
=E2=80=99s vision for a liberal global order. Then, after the radicalizing =
events of Trump=E2=80=99s first election, a worldwide backlash against soci=
al media, and the Covid-19 pandemic, they appeared front row at Trump=E2=80=
=99s inauguration, looking for all the world like loyal Republican acolytes=
.

Two years later, Silicon Valley=E2=80=99s tycoons are feeling blue. They=E2=
=80=99re making ham-fisted attempts to reconcile with the same Democrats th=
ey so recently rejected.

Will it work? There is a moral dimension to politics. Treating beliefs like=
 app features reads as cynical. A machiavellian perception of tech=E2=80=99=
s richest barons is one of the major reasons they suffered a significant re=
pudiation in the US midterms several weeks ago. But these mercurial CEOs ar=
e vital players in the US economy and national security, however much voter=
s may dislike them personally. Democrats as well as Republicans need Silico=
n Valley=E2=80=99s companies to grow.

