orgia, serif; font-weight: normal; margin: 0; padding: 0;" align=3D"left">
<strong style=3D"font-weight: 700;">This is just the beginning =E2=80=A6</s=
trong>
<br><br>
For two years, Americans have witnessed a largely unchecked assault on the =
foundations of US democracy. While enriching themselves through flagrant co=
rruption, the president and his allies have launched aggressive attacks on =
press freedom, voting rights, due process and many other fundamental civil =
rights. And they have shown a troubling disregard for the truth.
<br><br>
On election day, America=E2=80=99s embattled democracy came roaring back to=
 life: from Texas to Ohio to South Carolina, a majority of Americans reject=
ed this path at the ballot box. The voting public claimed its right to hold=
 power to account, and millions are now looking at politics with a new and =
unfamiliar feeling of hope.
<br><br>
The past two years demonstrated just how fragile our fundamental freedoms a=
re, when precious few powerful institutions are willing to stand up for the=
m. Major law firms and universities capitulated to a bullying president=E2=
=80=99s threats and intimidation. Some corporate news outlets bowed to his =
pressure, too.
<br><br>
In this atmosphere of fear, the independent press stood out as one of the o=
nly forces for accountability, dedicated to uncovering the truth =E2=80=93 =
and to making sure the public understood what was at stake in the election.
<br><br>
A free press has an essential role to play in a robust democracy, shedding =
light on hidden schemes and revealing the whole picture of our flawed world=
. Yet these are challenging times for press freedom: Trump=E2=80=99s crackd=
own on the press shows no signs of letting up; consolidated ownership means=
 fewer options and less diversity of perspective; and the AI industry has t=
hreatened the entire business model of journalism, resulting in extreme sta=
ff layoffs that have gutted newsrooms.
<br><br>
<strong style=3D"font-weight: 700;">We will need a free press in 2027 as mu=
ch as we relied on one in 2025 and 2026. The Guardian is fortunate to have =
no billionaire owner, no corporate bosses who put profit over mission. We=
=E2=80=99re accountable to the facts and to our readers. And the Guardian i=
s fortunate to have readers who are willing to support =E2=80=93 and commit=
 month after month =E2=80=93 to help sustain our independence.</strong>
<br><br>
